A||N||12||gmh||Starting problem????||||8||
Z||000000||gmh||01-09-2001||09:07 PM||||I need help, I have an 88 Fiero 4cyl, it has 126,000 miles, one year ago I had a new fuel pump put on and have not had trouble until the last couple of months. The past few months I have had a starting problem and after going thru the whole ignition system<BR>(New plug, plug wires, ignition module, starter) and still the same problem, also have went thru the fuel system, new injector, and diaphragm in pressure regulator. Well finally I am so frustrated and I took it to get a diagnostic, they tell me it is the fuel pump again, so now I have a new fuel pump again, with the same problem, I have not had a chance to test the old pump yet to see if it is bad or not, but I am going to this weekend. My computer shows no codes and the service station says the relay for the fuel pump is working and I also checked the fuse and it is good. The problem is random, in the morning it always has started, the problem has been when I have drove for a short distance and turned the car off, or when it has sat out all day at work and I come out to start it to go home. Has anyone had this problem, or any suggestions on what to check? The car runs great other than this glitch.<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-09-2001||09:24 PM||||I would think that they tested the fuel pump on the pressure that it was providing......<P>How old is the fuel filter?  If it is old and clogged, then possibly the fuel pressure is being restricted, causing your starting problem.<P>If that also was replaced when you went for the first new fuel pump, then I have no other ideas.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-09-2001||09:25 PM||||gmh,<P>It sounds as if you are applying good logic and are an intelligent mechanic.  I do not intend to insult you, but have you performed fuel pressure tests at the schraeder (spelling?) valve on a regular basis?  This should be a simple test to determine if the pump is in fact to blame.  Another idea would be rather simple as well... fuel filter.  Has it been changed recently?  These can cause fuel delivery problems.  I would check the pressure at the valve first.  Then you can narrow the problem to before or after the valve.  THe Before components are pressure regulator, filter and pump.  <P>Another question is have you tried to address the symptoms and not the problems?  My car 1987 EC likes to be primed before I start her.  I simply turn the ignition to the on position and wait for the fuel pump to kick off.  If I skip this procedure, she will rarely start.  My (until recently) 85 GT and 86SE do not require this priming.  For those of you that suspect the pump is going in my 87, I say pashaw!  I have owned the car for the last 50K miles and she has ALWAYS started better with the fuel priming.  <P>Hope this helps.... BTW Welcome to the Forum!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-09-2001||09:43 PM||||The 4 cylinder has no sschrader valve to tap into, only the V-6 does.<BR>Are you getting fuel up to the injector, and is it working properly?<BR>My '88 did that (long time ago) and the ECM was trashed, installed a different one and no problem since! (78K)<BR>Make sure the problem is not electrical (intermintent open) and try swappping it out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gmh||01-09-2001||10:00 PM||||The fuel filter was replaced one year ago when the changed the pump and then today when they changed the pump again, it was not clogged either time. I did check the fuel pressure line going into the throttle body and it showed good fuel pressure when the key was turned on. I have tried a bunch of combinations, letting the fuel pump prime first or just starting it right away, neither have helped. Usually if it won't start letting it sit for a while will work and then it will start right up.<BR>Would I get a code if the ECM was bad, I think it is like code 55? How would I check for a intermintent open? Also I have replaced fuel injector and seems to be operating good when car is running. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-09-2001||10:09 PM||||gmh,<P>When the ECM goes, it does not necessarily diagnose itself. That might be something you want to try, head to the local boneyard and pull the ECM from any 87-88.  Try to get one from the same trans.  OTW, you will need to swap out the prom.  This is prom swapping is heresay to me though... so if I am incorrect, someone please correct me.<P>Can you hear the pump running when the car will not start?  <P>Resto Guy,<P>Good point.  DOH!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000006||gmh||01-09-2001||10:30 PM||||Hope it is not the ECM, the fuel pump seems to be working even when I have starting problems, I have also looked in the throttle body for fuel and have seen none, that is what made me change the injector. Was wondering if the relay is intermitent or the oil pressure switch could be causing this.<BR>Thanks for the advice.||||reg||3
Z||000007||DJRice||01-09-2001||10:35 PM||||My brothers V6 Fiero was suffering a problem where it apparently would 'run out of gas'.  Occasionally, the Oil pressure gauge would bounce around.  It took quite a while before we figured out that the Oil Pressure sending unit was whacked out and would occasionally tell the ECM that there was no pressure which would disable the fuel pump.  <P>However, If your car starts and runs fine when cold, but wont start back up when things are heated up, I would definitely replace that relay.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000008||gmh||01-09-2001||10:52 PM||||I will have to change/check the relay, I kept asking the service station but they said it was good, my oil pressure gauge has always been unstable, most of the time it is pegged. Does anyone know how the relays come out?||||reg||1
Z||000009||gmh||01-10-2001||01:40 AM||||It died on me again, this time it started but it just seemed like it was not getting any fuel to the throttle body and it just died, I couldn't get it to start back up, the fuel pump relay seemed to run for longer than 2 seconds, I am going to have it towed back to the service station first thing in the morning.||||reg||9
Z||000010||RossT||01-10-2001||02:00 AM||||check out: <A HREF="http://www.fco.org/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fco.org/index.html</A> <P>click on "on line service guide"<P>scroll down a little and click on "starting problems"  then "no start" then "cranks but no start"  <P>good info about ecm problems||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiero Fanatic||01-10-2001||06:19 AM||||I had the same problem. It turned out to be the ignition wire to the starter solenoid. Every time the car got hot or I left the door light on, it caused the car to not start up. I replaced the wire to the solenoid and never had this problem again.||||reg||1
Z||000012||gmh||01-10-2001||10:23 PM||||Well new fuel pump relay, still same problem, drove around had stopped about 2 different times and it started right up, came home and let the car sit for a while and went out to start it and it would not start, waited about a hour and a half and it started right up. So back to the garage it is. I just don't get it.||||reg||9
